/* ---- TWO WORLDS (user Oct 31 20:41: "maybe story world should be a separate world from open world. Open world is more of a
   'everything' smashed together and I will be more tailored in the story mode. But I don't want to lose the open one (and
   battle mode uses it). So let's have a separate world for story"). Everything the editors keep of a world in this browser
   (WORLD_KEYS: the ground, the segments, the structures, the docks, the towers, the weather areas, the wrecks, the sky's and
   the background's settings...) has a second copy for STORY, under bd_sw_ in place of bd_. Which one the game reads is set
   before anything loads (bd_world: 'story', or none - the open world, OPEN WORLD, BATTLE and the rest): here, first of all,
   every read and write of a world key is sent to the Story copy when the Story world is the one loaded - on the page and in
   the simulation worker alike (its localStorage is the page's, copied - 00-sw-boot.js), so every file reads its own key as
   it always did. Changing worlds reloads the page (worldSwitch, from menuPlay): nothing of one world stays in the other.
   The Story copy starts as the open world as it was the first time STORY is chosen.
   (user Nov 9, iPad: BATTLE from a Story page was a black screen for 10 s - the whole page loaded again - before LOADING...) Changing worlds
   from the title is done in place now: STORY_WORLD changed and every world loader read again (worldReload - as an online guest's round
   takes the host's world), the simulation worker likewise at its next resync (swHostResync); the page is loaded again only from the open
   editor, or while another world is borrowed (online, the Biome Editor). ---- */
const WORLD_KEYS = ['bd_dock_own', 'bd_docks', 'bd_docks_x', 'bd_floor_pts', 'bd_ground', 'bd_islands', 'bd_lakes', 'bd_caves_conv', 'bd_crit_conv', 'bd_backgrounds', 'bd_bg_conv', 'bd_gshape_conv', 'bd_kraken_dy', 'bd_kraken_off', 'bd_kraken_u', 'bd_krakens', 'bd_land_pts', 'bd_land_structs', 'bd_land_towers', 'bd_structs', 'bd_stretch', 'bd_weather_areas', 'bd_owners', 'bd_plants', 'bd_world_ext', 'bd_wrecks', 'bd_sky_colours', 'bd_sky_p', 'bd_nature', 'bd_grade', 'bd_caves', 'bd_cave_params', 'bd_prices', 'bd_wbase', 'bd_air_conv', 'bd_sea_conv', 'bd_harbour_conv', 'bd_pads_conv', 'bd_ground_conv', 'bd_tower_conv', 'bd_air_ungroup', 'bd_placed', 'bd_gun_conv', 'bd_markers'];
const swKey = k => 'bd_sw_' + k.slice(3);
/* (user, Nov 1 14:05: "the snapshot holds any change from sky, background, properties, any property I change") EVERY bd_ key
   the SNAPSHOT carries is built in: each world's (WORLD_KEYS) in its world, every other one (the vehicles' tuning, the zoom
   levels, the editor's settings...) in WORLD_PACK.game, read by both worlds - all but LOCAL_KEYS, this browser's own
   bookkeeping (which world is loaded, the stamp its edits were made over, the Story copy's start, its save slots). */
const LOCAL_KEYS = ['bd_world', 'bd_wbase', 'bd_sw_init', 'bd_story_slots', 'bd_colv', 'bd_colv2', 'bd_scene_j', 'bd_veg_j'];   // (bd_veg_j: the Biome Editor's unsaved changes - 00-world-journal.js)   // (bd_scene_j: the editor's unsaved changes - 00-world-journal.js)   // (bd_colv: the DISPLAY tab's switches, this browser's own - 50-zz-editor-tabs.js)
const isGameKey = k => typeof k === 'string' && k.startsWith('bd_') && !k.startsWith('bd_sw_') && !WORLD_KEYS.includes(k) && !LOCAL_KEYS.includes(k);
/* (user Oct 31 21:38: "can we have a way that the snapshot has a timestamp? So if I get a build that has a snapshot that is more
   recent than my machine... if I change machines, then I'm always with the out-of-date version") THE BUILT-IN WORLDS: a
   SNAPSHOT the user pastes is put in here as it was kept (its store: each world's keys), stamped with when it was taken.
   A world key this browser hasn't kept reads the built-in one - a new machine, or one with no edits of its own, has the
   latest world at once, the artifact and the Pages alike. bd_wbase (a world key: each world its own) is the stamp of the
   built-in world this browser's edits were made over; a newer one built in, the title asks (worldStale): USE NEW (this
   browser's edits of that world let go) or KEEP MINE. Empty: nothing built in yet - the code's own defaults, as before */
// (WORLD_PACK: 00-world-pack.js - written by tools/bake-world.py from a pasted SNAPSHOT)
const worldPackOf = story => story ? WORLD_PACK.story : WORLD_PACK.open;
// a key as it is kept, whichever world is loaded (the SNAPSHOT: both worlds in one paste - 41-sky-new.js gameSnapshot)
let lsRawGet = k => localStorage.getItem(k), lsRawSet = (k, v) => localStorage.setItem(k, v), lsRawDel = k => localStorage.removeItem(k);
/* (user Nov 8 13:52, "See if it's a bigger issue": the last lake, the only placed vehicle, every marker deleted - back after a reload) a key removed
   (localStorage.removeItem - every saver's "nothing left": no lakes, no markers, every number at its default) while the built-in world has one is kept
   as LS_NONE: it reads as nothing (null - the table's own default), never the built-in one. Going back to the built-in world on purpose (REVERT ALL
   CHANGES, USE NEW) lets the key go: lsUnkeep (as the world names it) or lsRawDel (a stored name) */
const LS_NONE = '\u0000none';
let lsUnkeep = k => localStorage.removeItem(k);
let STORY_WORLD = (() => { try { return localStorage.getItem('bd_world') === 'story'; } catch (e) { return false; } })();   // (the BIOME EDITOR's own world comes in over whichever this is - vegIn: every world key read from it)
