// (user Nov 3 13:26: "when the plane is stalling, it kind of realigns itself? That should not happen. The plane should start
// free-falling and then the nose would aim downwards because of the weight, and then it would gain speed again") its body turns
// by the air on it, not with its path: the tail is a weathervane that turns the nose into the air it moves through - toward the
// angle of attack the elevator trims it to - as strongly as the air is fast (WV_K times its nose response at cruise, squared with
// its speed). So slow it hardly turns: stalled at the top of a climb it falls with its nose where it was, and as it falls faster
// the air from below swings the nose down into its fall, and it gains speed. FLY_ZETA: how soon a swing settles (1 none past);
