    // lifts less and gravity takes the nose down; upside down it lifts toward the ground and it falls out - user Nov 2 19:35: "He
    // should fall if I stop giving it speed"; "the tip of the nose doesn't feel heavier... like if gravity was pulling it down")
    const aT = ((F.vs / vc) * (F.vs / vc) - CF) * F.as;   // (flaps out: the angle that lifts the same - its trim holds)
    // (user Nov 3 16:27: "when you gain altitude ... the tilting is too snappy. I think it should be slower to lift the nose. You should
    // feel more resistance ... But when you're going down really fast and you want to stabilize the plane so you don't crash, it should
    // actually be easier, more forgiving. But I don't want this to be scripted") the stick moves the ELEVATOR, which sets the angle
    // the wings fly at - as a real one does: so much stick, so much angle, whatever the speed (it asked for a share of the most pull
    // before: every speed answered alike). The lift that angle makes - and so how hard the path bends - grows with the speed squared:
    // fast, out of a dive, a little stick bends it a lot; slow, climbing (its speed bleeding away), the same stick does little, more
