/* ================================================================
   OPEN WORLD (user Oct 27): what rests on the ground, and what's left of a plane that comes down on land
   - "Anything resting on the ground re-checks what's under it when the ground changes. If the ground has gone, it falls
     into the hole like the army wrecks already do. One shared step for all of them, run when the ground changes."
     The ground changes (a crater, sand sliding: ltDirty) -> landChanged wakes that stretch of it; each frame what rests
     in a woken stretch (or is still falling) is checked by restSettle: its ground gone, it falls - gathering speed -
     onto what's left, and tips with it (a thing as wide as the pad bridges a small hole, tips into a big one).
   - (user Nov 2: "Destroyed terrain should affect ALL vehicles - that is a global rule") every ground there is wakes its
     stretch the same way: the land's cells (ltDirty), a harbour's deck falling into the sea (pierAfterFall), a rock formation
     broken (rockBlast). A vehicle standing still looks at what's under it again only where its ground was woken (a parked car -
     storyCarBody, landWoke); one on the ground stepped each frame falls when it's gone from under it (groundFall: the
     helicopters set down, the planes on their wheels; the tank and the army's helicopter by their own falls, tankSettle /
     stepHeli).
   - Stones go whole (claude/ground-stones-rule.md, user Oct 26: "You can never slice a rock"): a crater takes a brown
     stone whole or knocks it loose, and a grey boulder it undercuts drops whole into the hole; each comes to rest and is
     bedded in the ground again where it lies.
   - The lake's water fills a hollow dug beside it, and the lake bed's pebbles and moss are drawn again on the new bed.
   - "When a plane crashes on land (e.g. 747) it currently just disappears? The hull should stay on the screen broken in
     pieces. As well as a big fire and smoke stack. Same with any other plane that crashes on land (and do a crater
     depending on the plane size)." (09:50): landCrash, one for every plane - the crater by its size, the hull broken
     where it hit and charred, the wings torn off beside it, a big fire and a tall column of black smoke (a minute for
     the 747, half that for a Cessna), then smoke as long again, then a smoulder; it stays for the round. Its fire sets
     nothing else alight yet (user 09:54: "We need to do some tests before doing fire spread").
   ================================================================ */
